WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.200
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.100
What languages does the Pope speak?
I want to talk about polyglot Popes.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.500
When you think of the Pope, you might think 
of a very Holy person, a religious person,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.580
someone who sits around praying all day
or something like that.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:25.060
But think about it, to be the leader of a huge organization 
like the Catholic Church, you have to be a very 
success-minded, driven and accomplished individual.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.300
So let's look at a few recent Popes 
and see where they rate on the polyglot scale.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:34.640
First, let's talk about the current Pope. 
Pope Francis who became the Pope in 2013.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.540
He is originally from Argentina and he speaks 
Spanish, fluently as his native language.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:48.220
He also speaks Italian fluently, he learned Italian 
as a kid when he was in Argentina from his father 
and his grandparents who were Italian immigrants.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:55.100
And early on, when he became the Pope,
at the beginning, he would only speak Italian in public 
for the media or in front of audiences.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.340
Italian is the lingua franca of the Vatican and it's 
the most commonly spoken language in the Vatican.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.320
And also he is the Archbishop of Rome.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.540
So it makes sense that he would speak Italian, 
the language of his own locality.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:14.380
I've also heard reports from Brazilian people
that, when Pope Francis visited Brazil, 
he spoke fluently in Portuguese.

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:16.700
Although, with a heavy Argentinian accent.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.720
And apparently, the Pope speaks also German 
and French, but is getting rusty due to a lack of use.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.680
Pope Francis also admits that he's had
quite a hard time learning English,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.780
in large part due to English phonology
 which he finds hard.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.120
During a three month stay in Ireland, when 
he tried to learn English, he had some trouble

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.300
Although I must say I can't blame him for being 
confused by the Irish people's funny pronunciation.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.960
Sorry Irish people, just kidding.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:46.620
After being the Pope for a little over a year, Pope Francis 
spoke English in public for the first time in Seoul, Korea.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.460
Are you ready to say yes?

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.900
- Are you ready? 
- [from the crowd] Yeah.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:01.160
So clearly Pope Francis is working on his English 
and he's improving it to be able to do his duties 
better around the world.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:07.140
Of course, he also knows Latin which is used for ceremonial 
purposes and for official documents in the Vatican.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.540
Part of his studies was also to learn biblical Hebrew 
and biblical Greek which are necessary for bible study.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:19.660
So Pope Francis speaks Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, 
some German, some French, some English 
and three literary languages.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.120
For a total polyglot score of 6 out of 10.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:28.380
That's pretty darn good by the way but he's up against
some stiff competition, so I can't give him 10 / 10.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:35.600
Next is Pope Benedict XVI who was the Pope 
from 2005 until 2013 when he resigned.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.660
He is known for being more of a legitimate polyglot 
than Pope Francis.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:46.320
His native language is German that he is also known 
to fluently speak Italian, French, English and Spanish.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.900
I've also read reports that he speaks Portuguese,
but not totally fluently.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.980
But he's very good at Latin and used Latin more 
than Pope Francis currently does as Pope.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:03.000
And, of course, he can also read biblical Hebrew and 
biblical Greek but, again, they're not spoken languages. 
Those are just classical written languages.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:09.760
Pope Benedict made waves in 2013 when he resigned 
as the Pope which is almost completely unheard of.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.460
And he made waves partly because 
his announcement was in Latin.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:22.080
The reporter who broke the story to the world ended up 
being the only reporter in the Vatican press office who 
could understand the original report in Latin.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:28.860
So let's recap for Pope Benedict XVI: he speaks German,
Italian, Spanish, French and English fluently.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:35.180
He also speaks some Portuguese and he speaks Latin and
he knows to literary languages, Greek and Biblical Hebrew.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.440
For a total polyglot score of 7 out of 10.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:47.220
But the king of polyglot popes is, without a doubt,
Pope John-Paul II, who was the Pope
from 1978 until his death in 2005.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:52.060
Pope John-Paul II was originally from Poland,
so his native language was Polish.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:56.640
But he was also fluent in Italian, Spanish, 
Portuguese, French and English.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:02.580
As well as Latin of course. Pope John-Paul II is 
also known for having a good working knowledge 
of some other languages.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.740
At least, enough to speak publicly 
in those languages, with some preparation.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:12.900
Those languages include Slovak, Russian, Ukrainian, 
Japanese, Tagalog, among many others.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:19.080
At the end of one Easter Mass, he once gave 
an Easter greeting in 57 different languages!

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.660
Maybe he loved language is so much,
because he was also an avid traveler.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:27.840
He visited 129 different countries, 
which is more than any other Pope in history.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.960
So to recap, Pope John-Paul II was fluent in:

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.280
Polish, Italian, Spanish, Portuguese, French, 
English and German. And Latin.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.580
And he knew a whole bunch of other languages, 
better than most language dabblers out there.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.660
So he gets a total polyglot score of 10 out of 10.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.920
Whether you're a Catholic or not, 
Pope John-Paul II was clearly an inspiring polyglot.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.600
And, as language lovers, we can look up to
and admire that aspect of his life and his work.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.380
Thank you for watching the Langfocus channel.
Have a good night.

